--  Answering for what went wrong.
--
--  Until a block could carry handlers, anything raised ended the submission:
--  a division by zero, a text that held no number, an index past the end of a
--  String. Each of them stopped the program, and there was no way to say what
--  should happen instead.
--
--  A handler answers for the exceptions it names. `others` answers for what is
--  left, exactly as it does among a case's choices -- and anything nobody
--  answers for goes outwards to a block that does.

--  The one every script wants: text that was supposed to hold a number.
function Read_Count (From : String) return Integer is
begin
   return Integer'Value (From);
exception
   when Constraint_Error =>
      return 0;
end Read_Count;

put_line (Read_Count ("42"));
put_line (Read_Count ("not a number"));

--  Chosen by name. Only the handler that names what was raised runs.
begin
   Word : String := "abc";
   Past : Character := Word (9);
exception
   when Constraint_Error =>
      put_line ("this one does not run");

   when Index_Error =>
      put_line ("that position is not there");
end;

--  A handler catches what a call raised, however deep: by the time it runs,
--  every frame between it and the raise is gone.
function Divide (By : Integer) return Integer is
begin
   return 100 / By;
end Divide;

begin
   put_line (Divide (0));
exception
   when Constraint_Error =>
      put_line ("cannot divide by that");
end;

--  And what a block does not answer for goes outwards. A handler that looked
--  at an exception and matched nothing must not swallow it.
begin
   begin
      Wrong : Integer := 1 / 0;
   exception
      when Index_Error =>
         put_line ("this one does not run either");
   end;
exception
   when others =>
      put_line ("the outer block answered for it");
end;

put_line ("and the program carried on");

--  A program may declare exceptions of its own and raise them. An exception
--  has no type, no value and no storage: what it is is a *name*, and a raise
--  and a handler agreeing on that name is the whole of the mechanism. The five
--  the machine raises for itself -- Constraint_Error, Program_Error,
--  Storage_Error, Tasking_Error, Index_Error -- may be raised by name too.
Wrong_Kind : exception;

function Weigh (What : String) return Integer is
begin
   if What = "" then
      --  Better than a status nobody checks: a caller that forgot to handle
      --  this stops, where a returned -1 would be passed on and arithmetic
      --  done with it.
      raise Wrong_Kind;
   end if;

   return What'Length;
end Weigh;

begin
   put_line ("weighed " & Trim (Weigh ("abc")'Image));
   put_line ("weighed " & Trim (Weigh ("")'Image));
exception
   when Wrong_Kind =>
      put_line ("nothing to weigh");
end;

--  `raise;` inside a handler raises again what that handler caught, so an
--  outer one still sees it. What it does not do is catch what it raised: the
--  handler's own guard is gone by the time its statements run, which is the
--  same rule that lets a handler report and pass a failure on.
begin
   begin
      raise Wrong_Kind;
   exception
      when Wrong_Kind =>
         put_line ("noticed here");
         raise;
   end;
exception
   when Wrong_Kind =>
      put_line ("and answered for further out");
end;

--  What is deliberately not here: `raise Wrong_Kind with "a message";`. Ada
--  attaches a string to the occurrence, and a handler reads it back with
--  Exception_Message. This language has no way to read one back, so the string
--  would be written and never seen -- and a message that nothing can read is
--  worse than none.
